#the /bin directory contains essential user binaries that are must be present when the system is mounted
# in single-user mode. Apps like FireFox, if not installed as snaps, are stored in /usr/bin.
# Important system programmes like bash are stored in /bin. The /usr directory can belong to 
#a different partition. Files stored in /bin directory ensure that the system will have these 
#important utilities even if no other file system is mounted. The /sbin directory is similar to /bin; it houses
#essential system administration binaries.


#/boot this directory contains the files needed to boot the system. For example, the GRUB boot loader's files
#and the Linux kernels are stored in this directory. However, the boot loaders config files are stored 
#elsewhere in the /etc directory where config files are stored.


#/cdrom is not part of the FHS standard, even though ubuntu and other os's have it. It acts as a temporary 
#location for CD-ROMS inserted in the system. The standard place for temporary storage is the /media directory.

# the /dev directory contains a number of special files that represent devices. They are not actual files as we
#know them, but they appear as files. For example, /dev/sda represents the first SATA drive in the system. 
# Opening a partition editor and editing /dev/sda  allows us to partition the /dev/sda SATA drive.
# the directory also contains pseudo-devices that correspond to virtual devices (not actual hardware ).
# examples include dev/random - which produces random numbers, and /dev/null - which produces
# no output and discards any input.

#/etc directory contains configuration files that can be edited by hand in a text editor. 
#/etc/ directory contains system-wide configuration files. User-specific config files are placed in the user's home directory.

#/home contains home folders for each user. For example, if a user is named bob, then there 
#is a home folder at /home/bob. The home folder contains the  user's data and user-specific configuration files.
#Each user has only write access to their home folder, and require elevated permissions (becoming the root)
# to modify other files on the system.

# /lib directory contains essential libraries needed by /bin and /sbin folders. Libraries needed by /usr/bin
#binaries are located in /usr/lib. There will also be a /lib64 folder counterpart on 64-bit systems.

#/lost+found - each linux file system has a /lost+found directory. If a file system crashes, then a 
#file system check will be perfomed during next boot. Any corrupted files will then be place in lost+found
#directory, allowing us to recover as much data as possible.

#/media - contains subdirectories where removable media devices inserted into the computer are mounted. 
#For instance, inserting a CD into the linux system will automatically cause a directory to be created
#insode the media directory. This new subdirectory contains the contents of the CD.

#/mnt - this directory is where sys admins mounted temporary file systems while using them. For Example,
# if you're mounting a windows partition to perform file recovery operations might mount /mnt/windows.
# It is possible to mount other file systems anywhere on the system.

#/opt - contains subdirecotries for optional software packages. It is commonly used by proprietary 
#software that does not obey the standard file system hierarchy. For example, installing a programme
# could dump its files in /opt/application.

#/proc - this directory does not contain standard files. Its files are special and represent 
#system and process information.

#/root - this is the home directory of the root user. Instead of existing
# in /home/root, it is located at /root. This is not the same as the system root directory '/'.

#/run - provides a standard place for applications to store transient files they require like sockets
# and process IDs. These files can't be stored in /tmp because files in /tmp may be deleted.

#/sbin - this is similar to /bin. /it contains essential binaries that are generally intended to be
#run by the root user for system administration.

#/snap - this directory is not part of the FHS. It holds snap packages and other files associated with snap.
#Ubuntu now uses snap by default, not every ditro is guaranteed to have this.

#/srv - this directory contains data for services provided by the system. If you were using the
#Apache HTTP server to serve a website, you'd likely store your website's files in a directory inside the /srv 
#directory. 


#/tmp - apps store temporary files here. These files are generally deleted whenever your system is restarted
#and may be deleted at any time by utilities such as systemd-tmpfiles. 

#/usr - contains apps and files used by users, as opposed to apps and files used by the system.
# For instance, non-essential apps are located inside the /usr/bin directory instead of the /bin directory
# and non-essential system administration binaries are located in the /usr/sbin directory instead of 
# the /sbin directory. Libraries for each are placed inside the usr/lib directory. The /usr directory also
#contains other directories. For example, architecture-independent files like graphics are located 
#in /usr/share. Also, locally compiled apps are installed in the /usr/local directory by default. This prevents
#them from mucking up the rest of the system.

#/var - is a writtable counterpart to the /usr directory, which must be read-only in normal operations. 
#Log files and everything else that would normally be written to /usr during normal operations are 
#written to the /var directory. For exampke, you'll find log files in /var/log.   
    
